WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.400
Hoino Circula turned 74,000 used car exports into 85,000 imports and a dirtier fleet. This

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:12.760
is unintended consequences, episode 166. Ozone emergencies and a fleet treated as fixed.

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:17.480
The weekday rollout and the early traffic counts. The second car buying that followed.

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:23.220
The exemptions and standards still in place. And a test for the next plate rule. Mexico City

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:28.480
officials introduced Hoino Circula amid worsening ozone and particulate levels that were triggering

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:34.660
frequent air quality emergencies. The observation they drew on was simple. Remove a share of cars from

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:40.320
the road on a given day, and total exhausts should fall. Vehicle ownership was rising quickly.

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:46.360
Public transit capacity was limited. No precedent existed for large-scale behavioral offsets.

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:51.920
The policy treated the fleet as fixed and assumed drivers would stay home or switch modes on their

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:57.080
restricted day. No one had yet measured how households might respond once the restriction became

00:00:57.080 --> 00:00:58.740
permanent rather than temporary.

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:05.120
Rational planners therefore expected measurable daily reductions in carbon monoxide, hydrocarbons,

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:06.460
and nitrogen oxides.

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:11.340
They calculated that a one-in-five reduction in weekday driving would translate directly

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:16.820
into lower concentrations at monitoring stations, because each vehicle removed meant one less

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:20.060
set of tailpipe emissions during peak ozone formation hours.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:25.540
The rural band most drivers from using their cars one weekday each week, according to the

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:30.580
last digit of the license plate, and the aim was to cut vehicle emissions on high smog days.

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:37.280
The band began on November 20, 1989, and applied to private cars during weekday hours inside

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:38.220
the federal district.

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:43.900
Exemptions were later added for newer vehicles that passed emissions tests, a feature intended

00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:45.400
to accelerate fleet turnover.

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:50.560
Planners wanted to reward owners who upgraded, assuming the older vehicles would gradually

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:53.360
leave the fleet rather than stay in use by new buyers.

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:58.360
early monitoring showed lower traffic counts on restricted days, and city statements

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:02.760
described the measure as an immediate success in easing congestion. Officials

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:06.700
tracked the first-year drop in observed vehicle volumes, and concluded the

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:11.140
arithmetic was working as planned. Skeptics noted that enforcement relied on

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:15.500
visible plate checks rather than continuous verification. The program still

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:19.160
expanded in reach, and it remained popular with residents who saw clearer

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:24.149
skies on their non-driving days. Households that needed daily mobility purchased

00:02:24.149 --> 00:02:28.249
second cars whose plates allowed driving on the restricted day. Because newer

00:02:28.249 --> 00:02:32.789
vehicles cost more, many buyers chose older, higher emitting models that were

00:02:32.789 --> 00:02:37.229
cheaper on the use market. Those older vehicles entered the city in large numbers

00:02:37.229 --> 00:02:41.229
because a lower purchase price offset the cost of the second registration and

00:02:41.229 --> 00:02:46.509
insurance. That price gap is what reversed the use car trade. The causal chain

00:02:46.509 --> 00:02:51.749
ran through the fixed plate-digit rule itself. Once permission to drive was tied to a permanent

00:02:51.749 --> 00:02:57.189
attribute of each vehicle rather than to its actual emissions or to total kilometers traveled,

00:02:57.749 --> 00:03:02.189
any household with sufficient cash could neutralize the restriction by adding one more car

00:03:02.189 --> 00:03:08.129
whose digit covered the band day. A 2008 study in the Journal of Political Economy documented

00:03:08.129 --> 00:03:13.389
that total vehicles in circulation rose and that the average emissions rate of the fleet increased

00:03:13.389 --> 00:03:21.109
rather than declined. Remote sensing data collected in 2022 later showed that model year 1994 to 2005

00:03:21.109 --> 00:03:26.669
passenger vehicles registered in the state of Mexico made up only 7% of the sampled fleet,

00:03:27.109 --> 00:03:34.249
yet produced 25% to 42% of measured carbon monoxide, hydrocarbons, nitrogen oxides, and smoke.

00:03:34.969 --> 00:03:41.049
Saturday expansions of the rule in 2008 produced no detectable air quality gains across eight pollutants,

00:03:41.049 --> 00:03:45.109
Consistent with households already covering a ban day by adding a car.

00:03:45.709 --> 00:03:50.669
Lower income owners who could not afford a second car face greater travel burdens, while

00:03:50.669 --> 00:03:54.029
wealthier households drove more often in exempt newer cars.

00:03:54.669 --> 00:03:58.929
Gasoline consumption rose, and the growth rate of public transit ridership slowed.

00:03:59.529 --> 00:04:05.489
The daily emission reductions originally projected at 15% or more failed to materialize on a sustained

00:04:05.489 --> 00:04:06.049
basis.

00:04:06.049 --> 00:04:11.969
When exemptions for newer cars were introduced, the same logic rewarded replacement among higher

00:04:11.969 --> 00:04:16.889
income owners, while the displaced older cars remained available for lower income buyers

00:04:16.889 --> 00:04:19.589
who still needed mobility on every weekday.

00:04:20.089 --> 00:04:24.069
The average age and the emissions rate of the fleet moved in the opposite direction from

00:04:24.069 --> 00:04:25.069
the planners forecast.

00:04:26.211 --> 00:04:31.611
Subsequent analyses including household surveys and remote sensing campaigns confirmed the shift

00:04:31.611 --> 00:04:36.671
in fleet composition and prompted discussion of low emission zones and accelerated electric

00:04:36.671 --> 00:04:37.631
vehicle incentives.

00:04:38.151 --> 00:04:43.151
The original weekday rule remains in force, now paired with verification programs that

00:04:43.151 --> 00:04:45.991
still exempt many vehicles from after 2005.

00:04:46.811 --> 00:04:51.971
National light duty emission standards have not been tightened since 2005, leaving older

00:04:51.971 --> 00:04:53.831
high-emitting cars on the road longer.

00:04:54.511 --> 00:04:59.611
Mexico City continues to set targets for further reductions, while that high-emitting slice of

00:04:59.611 --> 00:05:04.291
fleet still accounts for a disproportionate share of pollutants. Proposals for tighter

00:05:04.291 --> 00:05:09.351
age-based restrictions or higher verification fees have been discussed. Each new layer

00:05:09.351 --> 00:05:14.311
still meets the same arithmetic. Any rule that leaves one usable vehicle short on a given

00:05:14.311 --> 00:05:18.991
day creates an incentive to acquire another vehicle whose plate or exemption status fills

00:05:18.991 --> 00:05:24.271
the gap. What the first year counted was cars missing from the restricted day traffic volumes.

00:05:24.271 --> 00:05:28.571
times. What the plate digit rewarded was a second vehicle whose last digit restored

00:05:28.571 --> 00:05:33.651
the band day. Those are different objects. A drop in the count can sit beside rising

00:05:33.651 --> 00:05:38.991
gasoline use, slower growth in transit ridership, and a fleet whose average emissions rate

00:05:38.991 --> 00:05:43.851
has gone up. Planners who expected the one and five driving cut to appear at the monitors

00:05:43.851 --> 00:05:48.591
were following the observation they had. A car kept off the road as a tailpipe kept

00:05:48.591 --> 00:05:53.431
quiet during peak ozone hours. The missing measurement was what a household does once

00:05:53.431 --> 00:05:56.591
that quiet day is permanent, and a used car can buy it back.

00:05:56.871 --> 00:06:02.131
An exemption key to a newer car that passes an emissions test sorts owners by who can

00:06:02.131 --> 00:06:04.831
replace a vehicle, not by who stops traveling.

00:06:05.411 --> 00:06:07.411
The newer car earns the clearer calendar.

00:06:07.971 --> 00:06:11.711
The older car does not have to leave circulation for that exemption to be used.

00:06:12.111 --> 00:06:15.591
It can move to a buyer shopping on price who still needs every weekday.

00:06:16.151 --> 00:06:20.551
Verification then records a cleaner car in one household, and leaves a higher emitting

00:06:20.551 --> 00:06:21.891
car registered to another.

00:06:21.891 --> 00:06:27.331
A digit, an age line, and an emissions test can each be written so the cheapest legal response

00:06:27.331 --> 00:06:32.451
is to keep or acquire an additional high-emitting unit, rather than to reduce total travel.

00:06:32.971 --> 00:06:34.231
The trigger is doing the work.

00:06:34.651 --> 00:06:39.551
If permission lives on the vehicle, cash aimed at a second registration neutralizes the day.

00:06:39.991 --> 00:06:44.971
If the exemption rewards replacement without retiring the car that was replaced, the old exhaust

00:06:44.971 --> 00:06:46.971
stays in the fleet under a different name.

00:06:46.971 --> 00:06:52.011
Income decides who absorbs the travel burden and who drives the exempt car more often.

00:06:52.551 --> 00:06:55.991
None of that requires the original officials to have wanted dirtier air.

00:06:56.491 --> 00:07:01.091
It requires only that the rule, as written, made the second car the rational purchase.

00:07:01.791 --> 00:07:05.731
Environmental intelligence is where the monitoring question in a case like this belongs.

00:07:06.431 --> 00:07:10.811
Whether the pollutants at the stations are still falling after households have had time to adapt.

00:07:11.511 --> 00:07:16.271
How might a city test to propose restriction against the actual cost and availability of second

00:07:16.271 --> 00:07:21.171
vehicles before it is enacted. We'll leave it there for today. Thanks for thinking through

00:07:21.171 --> 00:07:25.891
this one with me. If you know someone who loves a good cautionary tale, send it their way,

00:07:26.291 --> 00:07:30.991
and I'll see you tomorrow with another. If this show earns a place in your day, follow it in your

